  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 318 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nA blocking high will remain anchored over the Great Lakes \nregion through Monday night. Central Illinois will stay situated\nbetween a Nor'easter tracking along the Mid-Atlantic and New \nEngland coasts and a strong shortwave trough pushing inland from\nthe eastern Pacific. This setup will maintain quiet, tranquil \nweather with seasonable temperatures, bringing daily highs in \nthe mid-70s and overnight lows around 50.\n\nThe synoptic pattern will rapidly evolve by Tuesday as a potent\ntrough carving through the southwestern U.S. merges with the \nremnants of Tropical Depression Polo. Strong shortwave energy \ndownstream of the main trough will eject across the Plains and \nthe Mid- to Upper-Mississippi Valleys, breaking down the \nblocking ridge and steering the Nor'easter out to sea. Robust \nthermal and moisture advection driven by this shortwave energy \nwill propel afternoon temperatures into the lower 80s on Tuesday\nand Wednesday.\n\nA key adjustment in recent forecast guidance is a delay in the \nonset of precipitation this week. Model trends increasingly \nindicate that as the parent southwestern shortwave trough phases\nwith Polo, it will pinch off into a closed upper low. This \nprocess will stall the surface cold front to our north and west,\ndelaying rain chances across central Illinois from Tuesday \nnight/Wednesday to Wednesday night/Thursday.\n\nThe evolution of the cutoff low over the Southern Plains will \nlead to two possible scenarios for central Illinois during the \nThursday to Friday timeframe. While both pathways offer high \nconfidence in widespread precipitation, subtle differences in \nfrontal timing and sharp temperature gradients will dictate the \nlocation and magnitude of the heaviest rain axis:\n\nScenario A (The slower/wetter solution): Features a weaker \nclosed wave positioned further west over New Mexico. This allows\na stronger upper-level ridge to hold over the eastern U.S., \npreventing a northern stream shortwave from digging south. The \nsurface front remains further north with a slower eastward push,\nplacing the heaviest rainfall axis over southeast Iowa and \ncentral Illinois.\n\nScenario B (The faster/drier solution): Depicts a stronger \nclosed wave farther east over Texas, which allows the northern \nstream shortwave and associated surface front to advance south \nthrough central Illinois more rapidly, thereby limiting total \nrainfall along the boundary.\n\nAnalysis of the global ensemble guidance shows slightly better \nclustering around Solution B. Consequently, caution is advised \nagainst QPF solutions forecasting widespread 2-3 inch rainfall \ntotals through Friday night, as actual accumulation may end up \nsignificantly lower.\n\nExpanding on these outcomes, both scenarios signal a mostly dry\nSaturday as surface high pressure rapidly builds across the \nMidwest and Great Lakes. This will bring a noticeable cooldown \ncompared to mid-week warmth, with high temperatures capping in \nthe lower 70s to close out the week.\n\nForecast uncertainty increases substantially late next weekend \ninto early the following week due to a highly chaotic synoptic \nsetup. Three main features will interact: the closed upper wave \nover the southwest U.S., a northern stream shortwave digging \ninto the central U.S., and a tropical feature lifting north out \nof the Gulf. The interplay among these system components remains\nuncertain, but it could produce a substantial swath of heavy \nrain somewhere between the Mid-Mississippi and Ohio River \nValleys by Sunday evening.",
